{#
 The LOGIN page.

 Copyright 2008 Steven Barth <steven@midlink.org>
 Copyright 2008-2012 Jo-Philipp Wich <jow@openwrt.org>
 Licensed to the public under the Apache License 2.0.

 It exists because the generic template includes the header WITHOUT `blank_page: true`: with no
 theme-local sysauth the whole chrome renders around the login form with every control dead.
 No departure here may drop a field the backend posts.
 Do not re-introduce a `<section hidden>` form that a view module reveals: the view bootstraps
 before a session exists, its RPCs answer "Access denied", render() never runs and the page is
 blank with no way to log in. The server renders the form, so it works with JS off.
 The strings carry no msgctxt: luci-base translates them in the ~40 languages without a catalogue.
-#}

{%
	/* Which router this is (openwrt/luci#8961): the login page has no chrome, so the only place
	   the name appeared was the browser tab. A second `ubus.call('system', 'board')`, because an
	   include cannot hand a local back from header.ut: one extra round trip on a page rendered
	   once per session. It discloses nothing new: the hostname already reaches an unauthenticated
	   browser through <title>, as in every stock theme. */
	const boardinfo = ubus.call('system', 'board') ?? {};
-%}

{% include('header', { blank_page: true }) %}

{#
	the class is the stylesheet's hook: the markup is ours, so the form is named rather than
	found by a 49-character `:has()` selector repeated 17 times, ~0.8 KB
#}
<form method="post" class="fs-login">
	{#
	 The router's name, above everything else in the card: an admin with three of these open has no
	 other way to tell the tabs apart once the form is focused. The `?? 'OpenWrt'` fallback and the
	 striptags/entityencode pair match head.ut and are load-bearing: the hostname is admin-controlled
	 text printed to an unauthenticated page. It is the card's visual title but a <p>, not a heading: the h1
	 `Authorization Required` below stays the one heading (clipped by .fs-sr), or a screen reader
	 hears the hostname as a section title.
	-#}
	<p class="fs-login-host">{{ entityencode(striptags(boardinfo.hostname ?? 'OpenWrt'), true) }}</p>
	{#
	 Two independent alerts: neither variable alone says which dispatcher branch rendered this.
	 `fuser` is set in both branches, including the OTP step after a correct password; and
	 `auth_message` (copied from get_challenges() BEFORE the check, dispatcher.uc:956) is set on
	 every pending result. `auth_plugin` is set only once session_setup() accepted the
	 credentials: gate the credentials alert on `!auth_plugin`, print the message beside it.
	 entityencode, where upstream prints raw: plugin markup travels in `auth_html`, so this is
	 text on an open page.
	-#}
	{% if (fuser && !auth_plugin): %}
		<div class="alert-message error">
			<p>{{ _('Invalid username and/or password! Please try again.') }}</p>
		</div>
	{% endif %}
	{% if (auth_message): %}
		<div class="alert-message{% if (auth_plugin): %} warning{% endif %}">
			<p>{{ entityencode(auth_message, true) }}</p>
		</div>
	{% endif %}

	<div class="cbi-map">
		{#
		 An h1, where every other LuCI theme leaves this an h2: the login page has no chrome, so
		 header.ut's `.fs-title-main` h1 never renders here and the document would have no top-level
		 heading for a screen reader. Clipped with .fs-sr, not shown: the hostname above is the
		 card's visible title and the words repeat what the fields say.
		-#}
		<h1 class="fs-sr">{{ _('Authorization Required') }}</h1>
		<div class="cbi-section"><div class="cbi-section-node">
			<div class="cbi-value">
				<label class="cbi-value-title" for="luci_username">{{ _('Username') }}</label>
				<div class="cbi-value-field">
					<input class="cbi-input-text" type="text" name="luci_username" id="luci_username" autocomplete="username" value="{{ entityencode(duser, true) }}" />
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="cbi-value cbi-value-last">
				<label class="cbi-value-title" for="luci_password">{{ _('Password') }}</label>
				<div class="cbi-value-field">
					<input class="cbi-input-text" type="password" name="luci_password" id="luci_password" autocomplete="current-password" autofocus />
				</div>
			</div>
			{#
			 The login backend's own fields: a pluggable backend puts its OTP input in `auth_fields`;
			 a template that drops them never posts it and nobody can log in with 2FA, so this one
			 stays a SUPERSET of the generic one's. The leading space in each conditional attribute
			 is load-bearing: ucode's lexer eats whitespace before `{%` and the newline after `%}`;
			 measured, the generic spelling renders
			 `inputmode="numeric"pattern="[0-9]*"maxlength="6"required`.
			 Every attribute value is entityencoded; maxlength goes through a template literal first, as
			 entityencode(6) is null.
			-#}
			{% if (auth_fields): %}
				{% for (let field in auth_fields): %}
					<div class="cbi-value">
						<label class="cbi-value-title" for="{{ entityencode(field.name, true) }}">{{ _(field.label ?? field.name) }}</label>
						<div class="cbi-value-field">
							<input class="cbi-input-text"
								type="{{ entityencode(field.type ?? 'text', true) }}"
								name="{{ entityencode(field.name, true) }}"
								id="{{ entityencode(field.name, true) }}"
								{% if (field.placeholder): %} placeholder="{{ entityencode(field.placeholder, true) }}"{% endif %}
								{% if (field.inputmode): %} inputmode="{{ entityencode(field.inputmode, true) }}"{% endif %}
								{% if (field.pattern): %} pattern="{{ entityencode(field.pattern, true) }}"{% endif %}
								{% if (field.maxlength): %} maxlength="{{ entityencode(`${field.maxlength}`, true) }}"{% endif %}
								{% if (field.autocomplete): %} autocomplete="{{ entityencode(field.autocomplete, true) }}"{% endif %}
								{% if (field.required): %} required{% endif %}
							/>
						</div>
					</div>
				{% endfor %}
			{% endif %}
			{% if (auth_html): %}
				<div class="cbi-value">
					{{ auth_html }}
				</div>
			{% endif %}
			{% if (auth_assets): %}
				{% for (let asset in auth_assets): %}
					{% if (asset.type == 'script'): %}
						<script src="{{ asset.src }}"></script>
					{% endif %}
				{% endfor %}
			{% endif %}
		</div></div>
	</div>

	<div class="cbi-page-actions">
		<input type="submit" value="{{ _('Log in') }}" class="btn cbi-button cbi-button-apply" />
	</div>
</form>

{%
	let https_ports = uci.get('uhttpd', 'main', 'listen_https') ?? [];

	https_ports = uniq(filter(
		map(
			(type(https_ports) == 'string') ? split(https_ports, /\s+/) : https_ports,
			e => +match(e, /\d+$/)?.[0]
		),
		p => (p >= 0 && p <= 65535)
	));
%}

{#
	The two server values the script below needs, handed over as DATA: a `{{ … }}` block is not JS
	until the server renders it, so this line carries no logic at all. The script itself is plain JS.
#}
<script>window.__fsHttps={ports:{{ https_ports }},resource:"{{ resource }}"};</script>
<script>
	(() => {
		/* If the router also serves HTTPS, hop over to it before the password goes over this
		 * connection; the <img> probe is upstream's trick for proving the TLS port answers first.
		 * The hop can arrive without the session: Chromium treats http to https as cross-site and
		 * withholds LuCI's `SameSite=strict` cookie, so a reader logged in over https lands back on
		 * this form (403 to the hop, 200 to curl with the cookie; Firefox sends it). Where this form
		 * is what the hop landed on, it is asked again once over the same scheme, which carries the
		 * cookie. The retry is bounded by the REFERRER, not a URL marker: a `#` marker never runs
		 * the retry on Firefox, so nothing takes the fragment back off the address. */
		if (document.location.protocol === 'https:') {
			/* the host is compared as a PARSED origin, not as a prefix of the string:
			 * `http://192.168.1.1.example.com` starts with `http://192.168.1.1` */
			const from = document.referrer ? new URL(document.referrer) : null;

			if (from && from.protocol === 'http:' && from.hostname === window.location.hostname)
				window.location.replace(window.location.href);

			return;
		}

		const { hostname, pathname } = window.location;

		(window.__fsHttps.ports || []).forEach((port) => {
			const url = `https://${hostname}:${port}${pathname}`;
			const img = new Image();

			img.onload = () => { window.location = url; };
			img.src = `https://${hostname}:${port}${window.__fsHttps.resource}/icons/loading.svg?${Math.random()}`;

			setTimeout(() => { img.src = ''; }, 5000);
		});
	})();
</script>

{% include('footer', { blank_page: true }) %}
